>> This trajectory allows a patient to be sent for tests more than once,
>> because you are exposing them to the branch decision more than once.
>> Is that what you want?
>
>
> Ah, i think i misunderstood rollback. I don't want patients to be sent for
> tests more than once. I thought that rollback (times=1) would stop that.

The "times" argument (infinite by default) defines the number of times
that a particular arrival would rollback upon reaching this activity.
It's equivalent to a do/for loop where "times" is the counter.

> I want some proportion to go for tests, the other proportion to be admitted
> or discharged.
>
> The people that go for tests are then admitted or discharged.
>
> I'm guessing rollback isn't the option here? Would the solution be to do two
> branches, one to do the tests (yes/no) and then all of them go to a second
> branch admitted/discharged?

Yes, a second branch is the solution. But I would define each
subtrajectory separatedly:

test <- trajectory() %>%
...
admit <- trajectory() %>%
...
discharge <- trajectory() %>%
...

and then,

patient <- trajectory() %>%
  branch(
    option=..., continue=...,
    admit,
    discharge,
    test %>%
      branch(
        option=..., continue=...,
        admit,
        discharge
      )
  )

Iñaki

